\description{
Perform k-fold cross-validation of \code{\link[=sphere_regression]{sphere_regression()}} over a grid
of kernel and penalty pairs \verb{(kernels, lambdas)}, using the mean
out-of-fold geodesic prediction error
\deqn{\widehat{\mathrm{CV}}(k, \lambda) =
  \frac{1}{n_{\text{test}}}\sum_{i \in \text{test}}
  d_{\mathbb{S}}\bigl(\hat\mu(X_i), Y_i\bigr)}
averaged over folds as the selection criterion. With
\code{elastic = TRUE} the criterion becomes the elastic shape distance:
predictions and held-out responses are lifted back to their native
representation and aligned with \code{\link[=align_vectorized_curves]{align_vectorized_curves()}} before
the distance is taken.
}
\details{
Candidate kernels are supplied as a \emph{list of functions} of two
arguments rather than as a bandwidth grid, so that families other
than the plain Gaussian one can be screened; \code{\link[=create_kernel_list]{create_kernel_list()}}
builds the usual radial-basis (plus linear) grid.

The reported \code{best_kernel} and \code{best_lambda} are the grid points
minimising the averaged criterion. Individual fits are wrapped in
\code{try(...)}, so a failure in one grid cell does not abort the sweep:
the corresponding entry of \code{mean_error} is averaged only over the
folds that succeeded, counted in \code{success}. Because a full sweep on
shape data can run for days, \code{partial_folds_fit} allows fitting only
a random subset of the folds, and \code{folds} allows reusing an
externally generated fold assignment across methods so that different
estimators are compared on identical splits.

Note that the low-rank reductions \code{subK} and \code{subY} are recomputed
per fold from the training data, but the response coordinate system
\code{iso} is set up once from the \emph{full} sample, so \code{subY} should be
understood as a computational device rather than as part of the
cross-validated pipeline.
}
